name w3-09-saturation-1
timeout 520
time 1991-01-19T00:00:00Z
wx fixture wx-2026-07-27T00Z.wxb

# CAMPAIGN W3, SORTIE 9 OF 10 - PACKAGE Q FAILURE MODE 3: MORE CONTACTS THAN SHOOTERS.
# Spec, anchor and sources: doc/campaigns/w3-desert-storm.md. Format: doc/missions/campaign.md.
# CONTROL: none. This sortie moves no lever; it is a SCALE measurement and its result is a set of
# telemetry columns, not a verdict.
#
# THE ANCHOR: the third of Package Q's failures was a COMMAND channel. The mission commander received
# "approximately 80 percent of the calls from all aircraft in the strike, an impossible workload" [T4],
# and the doctrinal consequence was smaller formations.
#
# WHAT FLIGHTBOX CAN AND CANNOT DO WITH THAT, STATED BEFORE THE FILE RATHER THAN AFTER IT (rule 7,
# re-checked against the tree on this round). THERE IS NO VOICE NET (gap C18). No aircraft can call
# another, there is no channel with a capacity, and nothing can therefore saturate. What EXISTS is one
# level down and is a genuine command picture with a genuine capacity: `pilot/FBFlightPicture`, the
# cooperative sort that assigns one target per member out of a shared picture. W5 measured it at
# two-ship scale and found two hard limits - a deliberate split collapses onto the nearest contact in
# 1.0 s, and a flight cannot sort two subjects that only one member each can see, because `Assign`
# matches every member against the COMPUTING aircraft's own contact list. THIS FILE ASKS THE SAME
# QUESTION AT FOUR-SHIP SCALE AGAINST SIX CONTACTS IN TWO GROUPS, which is the largest form the tree
# has. THE SUBSTITUTION IS NOT AN EQUIVALENT: a radio net that collapses under call volume and a sort
# that duplicates assignments are different failures, and the campaign does not claim otherwise. What
# it claims is that this is the ONLY saturable command object in the tree, and it measures it.
#
# THE ARENA is w3-07's, widened: eight F-16 in two flights of four (a strike flight against four aim
# points and a sweep flight), against six MiG-29 in two flights of three, all six under a GCI brief.
# Every coordinate is [SET]. `--elev const`, 0 m datum, no terrain masking (C4). `wx fixture` is the
# committed GFS blob and is a real atmosphere, not the anchor's.
# WHICH DEFENSIVE LAYER THIS FILE MEASURES: the FIGHTER layer. The surface layer is present (a P-18
# radiating from t = 0, a ZSU-23-4 that cannot reach 5 000 m) and briefed to hold.
# NIGHT: 03:00 local, sun about -48 deg [DERIVED]; the merge is eyeless, as every W3 merge is.
# THE SUBSTITUTION AND ITS DIRECTION are w3-07's: MiG-29 for MiG-23/MiG-25 makes RED STRONGER than
# history. No catalogue row flies as a combatant anywhere in W3.
#
# THE TWO ENGAGEMENTS ARE SEPARATED IN TIME (O1's rule): the four strike releases run from t ~ 175 s,
# the fighter merge from t ~ 195 s. With 14 fighters airborne the first flight-monitor K.O. is early and
# it ends the run for everybody, so every number below is reported with WHAT WAS STILL IN THE AIR at
# that instant beside it.
#
# THE ONE TACTICAL QUESTION: WITH MORE SHOOTERS THAN CONTACTS AND MORE CONTACTS THAN SHOOTERS AT
# DIFFERENT MOMENTS, DOES THE SORT STILL PRODUCE ONE TARGET EACH - AND WHERE DOES `flt_switch` EXPLODE?
#
# MEASURED (fingerprint 0f206445e4f38ef1):
#   the sort under load  four sweep members against six contacts. `flt_switch` peaks at 57 / 37 / 19 / 6
#                      for positions 1 / 2 / 3 / 4 - the LEAD re-sorts 57 times in 332.8 s, one change
#                      of mind every 5.8 s, and the number falls monotonically with position. `flt_dup`
#                      reaches 1 on ALL FOUR members: every one of them held a duplicate assignment at
#                      some point, and the cost function FLAGS it and never resolves it. `flt_free`
#                      peaks at 2 and `flt_src` is 1 throughout
#   the shooting       Blue: four AIM-120 solutions (t = 199.9 .. 209.1), one arrival at 7.40 m on
#                      `q9mia2` which does NOT kill. Red: five R-27R/R-73 solutions, one arrival at
#                      0.93 m which DOES - `q9str2` killed at t = 284.8
#   the strike         4 of 4 released (t = 172.5 / 188.0 / 203.5 / 219.0), all four aim points DESTROYED
#   the run            ends t = 332.8 on `q9str2`'s ground contact; 14 aircraft, 8.2 s of wall clock
# SO THE SORT DOES NOT COLLAPSE AT FOUR-SHIP SCALE - it CHURNS, and the churn is concentrated in the
# lead. That is a different failure from the anchor's saturated radio net and the campaign says so.
#
# WHAT TO READ:
#   telemetry_q9swp*.csv   `flt_src`, `flt_assign`, `flt_switch`, `flt_dup` - four members, six contacts.
#                          `flt_dup` > 0 is the duplicate the cost function flags and does not resolve
#   events.log             `flight SORT_ASSIGN` per member and per tick, `flight SPLIT` if a member
#                          loses its lead's station, `sms LAUNCH_SOLUTION` per shooter
#
# READING RULE: exit 3 = TIMEOUT or exit 1 = FAIL are both expected and NEITHER is the measurement.
# THE MEASUREMENT IS THE FOUR SORT COLUMNS ACROSS THE FOUR SWEEP MEMBERS, plus the number of strikers
# that reached release. A reader who reports a kill count has read a warhead's arrival geometry (O2).

net kari9
  control q9nod
  link wire
  period 4.0
  hold 3
  wcs free
  member q9nod
  member q9flk autonomy hold

unit q9str1
  module f16
  team friendly
  flight st9 1
  spawn 33.16000 43.85000 5000 90.0 450
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 80
  set fcr_mode off
  set attack_mode ccrp
  set rwr on
  set datalink on
  set store 3 mk84
  set store 7 mk84
  set cmds_mode auto
  set cmds_program 1
  set cmds_chaff 60
  set cmds_flare 60
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task attack
  wp 33.16000 44.36000 5000 450
  wp 33.16000 43.90000 5000 450
  objective kill unit q9tgt
  objective survive

